e of vigilance it takes  some time for your body and your mind to  come down  from that psychiatrist sarah levitt says  doing new things a little at a time can  help  it's when anxiety is out of proportion  with the threat  that it can become problematic we see  that from  sars that for some people it was years  afterwards that they  experienced mental health distress in  this case experts suggest  seeking out a professional back in  ottawa although stansberry says she's  vaccinated and wears a mask  even the idea of walking through a  crowded street is nerve-wracking  i'll get a little bit sweaty i'll feel a  little bit short of breath a physical  reaction  she hopes will subside with time jamie  morocco global news toronto  in winnipeg a 12-year-old boy is dead  after being stabbed during a fight  between two groups  it happened last night police arrived to  find the 12-year-old seriously injured  lying on the street he was transported  to hospital where he succumbed to his  injuries  i struggle with finding a word to  describe  this homicide specifically the  involvement of  the 12 year old that became deceased  there's a number of details involved  that are particularly troubling details  that we may be able to share  when our charges are laid there's some  details ultimately that will  perhaps come out when this is dealt with  in the court system  no charges have been laid yet but police  say their investigation has progressed  significantly coming up the coronation  of iran's new president  the subject of u.s sanctions and the  third major storm of the hurricane  season  pummels the gulf coast  tropical storm claudette soaked the u.s  gulf coast  the storm packed winds topping 70  kilometers an hour and dropped up to 250  millimeters of rain  on parts of louisiana mississippi and  alabama  where it also spawned a tornado the  system could affect atlantic canada  early next week  claudette is already the third named  storm of the atlantic hurricane season  which is forecast to be a busy one  iran's hardline chief justice will be  the country's next  president the election of ibrahim raizi  is widely seen as a coronation  because he's the supreme leader's  protege rajisi has been sanctioned by  the united states for his involvement in  the mass execution of thousands of  political prisoners  he may have won by 18 million votes but  there was voter apathy and it led to the  lowest turnout  in the country's history myanmar's  military is condemning a united nations  resolution calling for a global arms  embargo against the southeast asian  country  the rare resolution passed in the  general assembly friday  citing the military's excessive and  lethal violence  since carrying out a coup in february at  least 860 people have died in the  crackdown that followed  myanmar's foreign ministry says the  resolution is based on one-sided  sweeping allegations and false  assumptions  today demonstrators took to the streets  of the country's largest city  to call for the release of  democratically elected leader aung san  tsuchi  she's been detained since the military  seized power nearly five months ago  the protest coincided with her 76th  birthday  working from home and living in offices  still had the plan to fill calgary's  empty core  the pandemic has made working from home  the new normal for many canadians  as a result office buildings across  canada are empty  the latest data shows the office vacancy  rate hit 6.2 percent in vancouver  in toronto is just over 9 percent but in  calgary  it sits at a 32 percent vacancy rate  that city has been struggling to fill  office high rises  and as heather york's west explains  calgary has come up with a solution to  fill those empty spaces  with affordable housing it's called  sierra place a downtown calgary high  rise  that was once the busy office  headquarters for dome petroleum and  other companies  for years now it's been empty part of  the city's growing glut  of vacant office space unbelievably bad  you know i feel like i've said over the  last number of years that the downtown  vacancy problem is unbelievably bad  it's bad now in fact in 2020 calgary's  downtown vacancy rate hit a record high  of nearly 27 percent  and it's not expected to get better  anytime soon there are 16  buildings in the city that are currently  completely vacant  thanks to both the energy sector  downturn and covet 19.  but in this 10-story tower a  transformation is underway  our vision for this project is a  multi-generational family hub  and what this represents to families who  are experiencing homelessness  or on the edge of homelessness is it  represents hope  over the next year these former board  rooms and law offices  will become studio one or two-bedroom  apartments  affordable housing units in a city with  a desperate need for more  it's the first of its kind project in  alberta and a test case many  eyes are on i think there are a lot of  people and a lot of  a lot of levels of government and  partners across the country who are  going to be watching this  calgary city council is hoping the idea  will catch on  in addition to the 5.5 million dollars  in support it's promised  this project the city is also  establishing a 45 million dollar fund  it hopes will entice developers to  remake other vacant office towers as  well  we've had one purely private sector  conversion this is our first attempt at  an affordable housing conversion  it is a bit of an experiment to see how  well it goes but certainly there are  buildings downtown calgary that have  very good bones  a potential solution to two problems  that post pandemic  are certainly not calgary's alone  heather eric's west global news  calgary to elephant watch in china  the now famous herd that's trekked  hundreds of kilometers  could be heading home the team that's  been tracking the 15 elephants says the  herd has shown a trend of returning  south  but scientists still can't figure out  the herd's specific route  the elephants left their wildlife  reserve in march of 2020 that was 15  months ago  their epic road trip has received  international attention  up next the official end of slavery is  officially a u.s holiday  [Applause]  the group black lives matter held a  rally outside prime minister justin  trudeau's office demanding changes to  the justice system  and renewing a call to defund the police  the demonstration  included families of black and  indigenous people who died  during interactions with police that  event took place as americans mark  juneteenth the anniversary of the end of  slavery in the united states  on june 19 1865 enslaved black people in  galveston texas learned  the civil war was over and the  emancipation proclamation  had freed them two and a half years  earlier more than 150 years later  juneteenth is now finally a federal  holiday  here's jennifer johnson  [Music]  from boston to san francisco juneteenth  is finally being celebrated across the  u.s  some people don't realize that  juneteenth is america's story they think  it's solely a black story this is a  story of all america  on this day in 1865 union army general  gordon granger came to galveston texas  and informed the last american slaves  they were free  two months after the civil war had ended  and over two years after the signing of  the emancipation proclamation  think about that for more than two years  the enslaved people of texas were kept  in servitude  after swift passage in both the house  and senate u.s president joe biden  signed a law  making juneteenth an official national  holiday this is a day  profound in my view profound weight and  profound power  many americans even those in galveston  never knew of june 19's history  why artist reginald adams painted this  mural in the city  i want people to be inspired and  hopefully learn something that they  didn't know before they saw the work of  art  black americans have waited decades for  this holiday to be recognized on a  federal level  nine states had already designated  juneteenth as an official paid state  holiday  june many others had resolutions and  proclamations  only south dakota does not mark the day  in any form  but after the death of george floyd and  demonstrations that followed  there was a renewed spotlight on racial  injustice in america  and a greater push to remember this  important day in history  we were all in the same valley of  suffering together  the sand was shifting underneath all of  us at the same time  the biden administration is being  praised for its action with some fellow  lawmakers taking additional steps  requiring the history of this day to be  taught in schools  with this change the people of illinois  will have a day to reflect  on how the freedom that we celebrate  just  two weeks later on the fourth of july  was delayed  to black americans now all americans  will at least recognize the name of this  holiday  while many others will have an official  day to celebrate it  jennifer johnson global news washington  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill tonight  your canada is this field of horses near  gore bay on manitoulin island in ontario  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  you
 

